      'en': 'Answer: <b>ことに なりました</b>. The parenthetical makes clear it is <b>the company\'s decision</b>: not your own choice but an arranged outcome, so use <b>ことになる</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>ことに しました</b> is your own decision; <b>ものです</b> is common sense or a feeling; <b>とのことです</b> reports what someone else said, but the subject is 「私」, and you wouldn\'t use a hearsay form for your own transfer.<br>Learn them as a pair:<br>　・<b>〜ことにする</b> = <b>I decide</b> (personal will)<br>　・<b>〜ことになる</b> = <b>it has been decided / it turned out this way</b> (objective outcome)<br>Japanese speakers often use ことになる to <b>play down their own role</b>: even when they chose something themselves, they say 「〜ことになりました」, which sounds less self-assertive.',
